1
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:08,740
Sami thinks a dice is biased.

2
00:00:08,790 --> 00:00:11,660
He rolls it 50 times, and gets 14 sixes.

3
00:00:11,710 --> 00:00:15,050
What would you expect from a fair dice?

4
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:20,740
How many sixes would you expect in 50 rolls of a fair dice, to 1 decimal place?

5
00:00:20,790 --> 00:00:24,440
Pause the video and work it out.

6
00:00:24,490 --> 00:00:26,710
1 sixth of 50 is about 8.3.

7
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:28,800
He got 14, which is more.

8
00:00:28,850 --> 00:00:31,700
But is that enough evidence?

9
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:37,660
Remember: relative frequency is the frequency divided by the number of trials.

10
00:00:37,710 --> 00:00:44,020
Sami keeps rolling, and records the relative frequency as the number of rolls grows.

11
00:00:44,070 --> 00:00:44,780
Your turn.

12
00:00:44,830 --> 00:00:48,370
What is the relative frequency after 500 rolls?

13
00:00:48,420 --> 00:00:51,830
Pause the video and try it.

14
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:54,330
84 divided by 500 is 0.168.

15
00:00:54,380 --> 00:00:58,320
That is very close to 1 sixth, about 0.167.

16
00:00:58,370 --> 00:01:01,640
The dice looks fair after all.

17
00:01:01,690 --> 00:01:03,540
Small samples can mislead.

18
00:01:03,590 --> 00:01:06,720
Large samples give better estimates.

19
00:01:06,770 --> 00:01:12,550
Sometimes there is no theoretical probability, like a drawing pin landing point up.

20
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:14,860
Then you must experiment.

21
00:01:14,910 --> 00:01:15,530
Your turn.

22
00:01:15,580 --> 00:01:23,480
What is the best estimate of the probability a pin lands point up: the relative frequency from many drops, or a half?

23
00:01:23,530 --> 00:01:26,040
Pause and choose.

24
00:01:26,090 --> 00:01:28,670
The relative frequency from many drops.

25
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:31,850
There is no reason for it to be a half.

26
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:36,990
Expected frequency is the probability times the number of trials.

27
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:38,390
Here is the method.

28
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:41,590
Step 1: find the relative frequency.

29
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:47,090
Step 2: compare it with the theoretical probability, if there is one.

30
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:50,030
Step 3: think about the number of trials.

31
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:52,150
More is more reliable.

32
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:55,710
Step 4: use the best estimate to predict.

33
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:58,330
A spinner has 4 equal sections.

34
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:01,620
In 1,000 spins, red comes up 380 times.

35
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:04,230
A fair spinner gives about 250.

36
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:07,650
It is probably biased towards red.

37
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:11,820
A pin lands point up 62 times in 200 drops.

38
00:02:11,870 --> 00:02:13,860
The estimate is 0.31.

39
00:02:13,910 --> 00:02:17,620
In 1,500 drops, expect about 465.

40
00:02:17,670 --> 00:02:21,500
Combine results for a better estimate.

41
00:02:21,550 --> 00:02:27,460
9 sixes in 60, and 41 in 240: 50 in 300, about 0.167.

42
00:02:27,510 --> 00:02:29,990
Sami got 14 sixes in 50 rolls.

43
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:32,230
Which line is wrong?

44
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:33,460
Line 2 is wrong.

45
00:02:33,510 --> 00:02:36,390
50 rolls is too few to be sure.

46
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:42,600
After 500 rolls, the relative frequency was 0.168, close to fair.

47
00:02:42,650 --> 00:02:49,450
Medical trials test new treatments on thousands of people, so that the results are reliable.

48
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:50,890
One last question.

49
00:02:50,940 --> 00:02:55,020
The probability is 0.31, and there are 1,000 trials.

50
00:02:55,070 --> 00:02:56,850
How many do you expect?

51
00:02:56,900 --> 00:03:00,400
Pause the video and answer.

52
00:03:00,450 --> 00:03:04,520
0.31 times 1,000 is 310.

53
00:03:04,570 --> 00:03:09,510
To remember: relative frequency is frequency divided by trials.

54
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:13,780
With many trials, it settles near the true probability.

55
00:03:13,830 --> 00:03:16,930
Expected frequency is P times trials.

56
00:03:16,980 --> 00:03:19,930
Do not judge bias from a few trials.

57
00:03:19,980 --> 00:03:25,390
Great work! Now practise lesson 12.4 on Camday.
